vm-7 — CLOS dispatch in bytecode + inline caches (plan-2-bytecode-vm).

Make generic-function calls fast — the dominant cost in method-heavy code (ASDF).

WHAT
- A CALL-GENERIC opcode (or CALL-NAMED recognising a generic function) that:
  computes the argument classes, and consults an INLINE CACHE attached to the call
  site (a slot in the constant pool / a per-callsite cache object):
    monomorphic: cache the last (class-key -> effective-method); on hit, jump
      straight to the effective method (a compiled function).
    polymorphic: a small n-way cache; miss -> full COMPUTE-APPLICABLE-METHODS +
      method-combination, then fill the cache.
- Effective methods compiled to bytecode where possible (CALL-NEXT-METHOD as a
  captured continuation / next-method chain).
- Interacts with the CLOS class hierarchy (kernel CLASS vs CLOS %cls duality, see
  [[bocl-class-duality-and-quirks]]): the cache key is the argument classes'
  identity; invalidate caches on class redefinition / add-method / remove-method.
- Fallback: if a generic function or its methods are not compiled, dispatch through
  the existing tree-walked CLOS path (via the uniform APPLY, vm-6).

ACCEPTANCE: a small class lattice with methods + method-combination; measure the
per-call cost with vs without the inline cache; correctness under add-method /
redefinition (cache invalidation); parity with the tree-walked CLOS on the CLOS
tests.

DEPENDS ON: vm-6.  Optional acceleration; can ship the VM first and add this.
